# A Markdown subset

### A renderer you can read whole

Tutors Ladder, rung 2 (Lite), talk 2

---

## Why write one at all?

Upstream renders Markdown with **markdown-it** and ten plugins (`packages/jsr/model/src/utils/markdown-utils.ts`):
anchors, a table of contents, emoji, sub, sup, mark, footnotes, definition lists, a video player, a podcast
player, KaTeX for math (`loadMath`), and a Shiki highlighter that a shell hands in (`initHighlighter`).

Rung 2's fence forbids any dependency. A Markdown library arrives in rung 4 (Reader). So rung 2 has
`src/markdown.ts`: about seventy lines that handle what Tutors courses mostly use, and show the rest as text.

You will understand every line. That is the point of the rung.

---

## What it renders

| Markdown | HTML |
| --- | --- |
| `# Title` to `###### Title` | `<h1>` to `<h6>` |
| lines separated by a blank line | `<p>`, lines joined with a space |
| `- a`, `* a`, `+ a` | `<ul><li>` |
| `1. a`, `1) a` | `<ol><li>` |
| a code fence with three backticks or tildes, with a language | `<pre><code class="language-ts">` |
| `**bold**`, `*em*`, `_em_` | `<strong>`, `<em>` |
| a code span in backticks | `<code>` |
| `[text](url)`, `![alt](src)` | `<a href>`, `<img>` |

Everything else is a paragraph of escaped text. Nothing is ever dropped.

---

## Blocks: one loop, one line at a time

```ts
// variants/rung-2-lite/src/markdown.ts
export function renderMarkdown(markdown: string): SafeHtml {
  const lines = markdown.replace(/\r\n?/g, "\n").split("\n");
  const out: string[] = [];
  let i = 0;
  while (i < lines.length) {
    const line = lines[i]!;
    if (line.trim() === "") i++;
    else if (fence)                            { /* collect to the closing fence, escape it all */ }
    else if (heading)                          { /* one line */ }
    else if (BULLET.test(line) || NUMBERED.test(line)) { /* collect matching lines */ }
    else                                       { /* a paragraph, until a blank line or a new block */ }
  }
  return new SafeHtml(out.join("\n"));
}
```

Each branch starts at line `i`, consumes the lines it owns, and leaves `i` at the next unclaimed line.
Adding a block (a table, a quote) means adding one branch, before the paragraph.

---

## Code fences come first

```ts
const FENCE = /^(```|~~~)\s*([\w+-]*)/;

} else if (fence) {
  const code: string[] = [];
  for (i++; i < lines.length && !lines[i]!.startsWith(fence[1]!); i++) code.push(lines[i]!);
  i++; // the closing fence
  const language = fence[2] ? ` class="language-${escapeHtml(fence[2])}"` : "";
  out.push(`<pre><code${language}>${escapeHtml(code.join("\n"))}</code></pre>`);
}
```

Inside a fence nothing is Markdown: `**not bold**` stays as written, and every character is escaped. The
closing fence must use the same marker it opened with.

---

## Paragraphs end where a block begins

```ts
const startsBlock = (line: string) => FENCE.test(line) || HEADING.test(line) || BULLET.test(line) || NUMBERED.test(line);

const paragraph: string[] = [];
for (; i < lines.length && lines[i]!.trim() !== "" && (paragraph.length === 0 || !startsBlock(lines[i]!)); i++)
  paragraph.push(lines[i]!.trim());
out.push(`<p>${renderInline(paragraph.join(" "))}</p>`);
```

A paragraph takes lines until a blank line or a line that starts another block. So a list straight after a
sentence is still a list. CommonMark has the same idea, with many more cases.

---

## Inline: tokens first, emphasis second

```ts
/** Code spans, images and links: rendered whole, so emphasis never reaches inside a URL. */
const TOKEN = /(`[^`]*`|!\[[^\]]*\]\([^)\s]*\)|\[[^\]]+\]\([^)\s]*\))/;

export function renderInline(text: string): string {
  return text.split(TOKEN).map((part, i) => {
    if (i % 2 === 0) return emphasis(escapeHtml(part));          // plain text
    if (part.startsWith("`")) return `<code>${escapeHtml(part.slice(1, -1))}</code>`;
    const [, bang, label, url] = part.match(/^(!?)\[([^\]]*)\]\(([^)\s]*)\)$/)!;
    return bang ? `<img src="${escapeHtml(url!)}" alt="${escapeHtml(label!)}">`
                : `<a href="${escapeHtml(url!)}">${emphasis(escapeHtml(label!))}</a>`;
  }).join("");
}
```

`split` with a capturing group keeps the tokens at the odd indexes. Plain text is at the even ones.

---

## Why the order matters

```ts
assert.equal(renderInline("[x](https://a.dev/a_b_c)"), '<a href="https://a.dev/a_b_c">x</a>',
  "emphasis never reaches inside a URL");
assert.equal(renderInline("snake_case_name"), "snake_case_name");
assert.equal(renderInline("`a *b* c`"), "<code>a *b* c</code>");
```

If emphasis ran first, `a_b_c` in a URL would become `a<em>b</em>c`, and a code span would lose its
asterisks. Cutting the tokens out first means each rule sees only the text it owns.

And `_em_` needs a non-word character on each side, so `snake_case_name` is left alone.

---

## Escape first, then add tags

```ts
function emphasis(escaped: string): string {
  return escaped
    .replace(/\*\*([^*]+)\*\*/g, "<strong>$1</strong>")
    .replace(/\*([^*]+)\*/g, "<em>$1</em>")
    .replace(/(^|\W)_([^_]+)_(?=\W|$)/g, "$1<em>$2</em>");
}
```

`emphasis` takes **escaped** text. Every `<`, `>`, `&` and quote of the input is already an entity, so the
only tags in the output are the ones the renderer adds. That is why `renderMarkdown` may return `SafeHtml`.
The next talk is about that promise.

---

## The tests are the specification

```ts
// variants/rung-2-lite/tests/markdown.test.ts
test("headings, paragraphs and lists", () => {
  assert.equal(md("# Title\n\nOne\ntwo\n\n## Next"), "<h1>Title</h1>\n<p>One two</p>\n<h2>Next</h2>");
  assert.equal(md("- a\n- b\n\n1. c\n2. d"), "<ul><li>a</li><li>b</li></ul>\n<ol><li>c</li><li>d</li></ol>");
});

test("code fences keep their text and name their language", () => {
  assert.equal(md("```ts\nconst a = 1;\n**not bold**\n```"),
    '<pre><code class="language-ts">const a = 1;\n**not bold**</code></pre>');
});
```

One input, one exact output. When you add tables in the assignment, the grading test is written the same way,
with markdown-it's output as the oracle.

---

## What it leaves out, and what that costs

| Left out | What an author sees |
| --- | --- |
| tables | the pipes, as a paragraph (the assignment fixes this) |
| block quotes, horizontal rules | the `>` or `***`, as text |
| nested lists | a flat list |
| raw HTML in Markdown | the tags, escaped, as text |
| setext headings, reference links, footnotes | the source, as text |
| math, Mermaid, highlighting | the source, in a paragraph or a code block |

Every gap shows the author's source, escaped. A gap is visible, never dangerous.

---

## Upstream allows raw HTML

```ts
// packages/jsr/model/src/utils/markdown-utils.ts (tutors-mono-repo 2b96b69)
export const markdownIt: MarkdownItInstance = new MarkdownIt({
  html: true, // Enable HTML tags in source
  ...
});
```

With `html: true`, a tag in a note reaches the page as a tag. Authors can use `<details>` or a coloured
`<span>`. They can also, by accident or on purpose, put a script on the page.

Rung 2 chose the other side of that trade. The talk on escaping shows the difference on the same note, and
rung 6 (Guarded) shows how upstream's reader sanitises.

---

## Summary

- a block loop and an inline tokenizer, about seventy lines, no dependency
- tokens before emphasis, escaping before tags
- anything unknown is shown as escaped text
- the tests are exact input and output pairs

Next: escaping by default, everywhere.
